Yes, I agree with the bit about not being a 1-to-1 mapping of symbols
to sounds, especially in Chinese where each symbol is a *word*, not a
sound.  Phonetically poor?  Chinese isn't phonetic AT ALL.  If you see
a word you don't know you have no way of figuring out how to pronounce
it.  That's why Chinese school textbooks for younger kiddies have
pinyin (pronounciation guide using Roman characters) pronounciations
printed over every word. (So why bother with the whole character thing
when you can just write everything in pinyin, you ask?  Well, let me
begin by explaining how just about every Chinese word has at least one
commonly-used homophone...)

Now what's wrong with stuff like pinyin and Kangi?  They're consistent
(because the languages they come from are very consistent and don't
contain any phonetic mix-ups like their silly European cousins) and
they function fine when used on their own language.  If foreign words
get a little mutilated during transliteration then so be it.  Sounds
in Asian languages mostly end in vowels anyway, so if *you* don't add
them while translating the Asian language speakers will do it for you.

Just my thoughts

